+# Summary
+
+## Changed
+- `confkit/parse.py`: replaced the `inilite` wrapper with an in-repo parser. `loads()` and `load()` follow `docs/FORMAT.md`. That covers sections, dotted keys, quoted strings, multi-line values, comments, typed scalars, lists, `@include` (cycle detection by real path, per-file `source`), duplicate and conflict checks, and `ParseError` line numbers.
+- `tests/test_parse_extra.py`: new tests for scalars, BOM and line endings, list errors, conflicts, continuation errors, includes, and `load()` of a missing file raising `OSError`.
+- No existing test, `vendor/`, or any file outside `confkit/`, `tests/` and `SUMMARY.md` was changed. `vendor/inilite` was not imported and no code was copied from it. No new dependencies.
+
+## Verified
+- `python -m unittest`: 20 tests ran, all OK. That is the 13 existing tests plus 7 new ones. The existing tests no longer error at import.
+- `python -m confkit /tmp/t.ini --get a.b` printed the JSON list `[1, 2]`.
+- `python -m confkit /tmp/t.ini --get a.z` printed `no such key: a.z` to stderr and exited 2.
+- `python -m confkit README.md` (not a valid config) printed `README.md:3: invalid line`. I read the exit code of this run only from the output, not from `$?`.
+- Spot checks of about 25 inputs, run with `loads()` in a throwaway script, matched my reading of the spec. They covered list edge cases, number forms, duplicate and conflict cases, and escapes.
+
+## Not verified or unsure
+- Where the spec is ambiguous, I made these choices:
+  - A quoted value with continuation text after it gives `unexpected text after string`.
+  - A bad string or escape in an `@include` path gives that string error, not `invalid include`.
+  - `[a]#c` (comment directly after `]`, with no space) is accepted.
+  - A trailing backslash at the end of a line inside a string gives `unterminated string`.
+  - If the first line of a value is empty or only a comment, the value type comes from the first non-empty continuation line.
+- The `ParseError` message text after the required prefix is my own wording.
+- I did not test symlinked include loops, include cycles more than two files deep, a non-UTF-8 include file, or an include path that is a directory.
+- `python -m confkit` on a missing file prints a traceback (`OSError`). The spec says `load` raises `OSError` and `__main__.py` is outside what I may change. I did not alter that.
+- I did not measure performance.
